1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,400
Russia's engineering feats have dazzled the world.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,240
One was as much a miracle as a tribute to human toil,

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:10,840
the Trans-Siberian Railroad.

4
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:14,480
It was the most expensive, difficult, and longest railroad

5
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:18,680
ever constructed, running 6,000 miles across Asia

6
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:20,180
to the Pacific.

7
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:22,380
And every mile was built by hand.

8
00:00:46,054 --> 00:00:54,900
Moscow, Russia's capital, and the seat of the former Soviet Union, is the hub of one of the largest railroad networks in the world.

9
00:00:54,901 --> 00:00:57,540
There are nine railway stations in Moscow

10
00:00:57,640 --> 00:00:59,540
that route a million passengers a day

11
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:03,300
to and from Europe and Asia.

12
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:06,480
In this vast land, trains carry more passengers

13
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:10,680
than any other means of transportation.

14
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:13,960
Yaroslavsky Station, built under Joseph Stalin,

15
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:16,820
is the western terminus for the most famous train

16
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:20,200
in the country, the Trans-Siberian Railway.

17
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:23,500
It makes 45 stops over an eight-day trip

18
00:01:23,501 --> 00:01:28,900
trip that runs 6,183 miles over, across,

19
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,740
and through a continent synonymous with frigid

20
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:35,880
temperatures and impassable terrain.

21
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:39,480
Along the way, it is everything a train can be,

22
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:42,480
a school bus, a taxi, a job.

23
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:48,300
Most of all, it is an essential link to villages

24
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:51,160
like Vizhvianca on the fringe of civilization

25
00:01:51,161 --> 00:01:53,560
in the heart of Central Asia.

26
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:55,800
To the people who depend on this train,

27
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:58,860
there is nothing extraordinary about the sight of tracks

28
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:01,740
and tunnels carved through the sheer granite cliffs

29
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:05,180
that rise 1,500 feet above Lake Baikal,

30
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:07,880
where ice lingers till May and temperatures

31
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:10,780
drop to minus 40 in February.

32
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:13,920
The railroad has been here since 1905.

33
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:16,520
No one remembers life without it.

34
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:21,160
Like a heartbeat, it is quietly taken for granted.

35
00:02:21,161 --> 00:02:29,740
Yet a century ago, it was virtually unthinkable that a train would unite Europe and Asia.

36
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:33,140
Here, the terrain and climate are treacherous.

37
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:36,780
The job was enormous, and the costs were staggering.

38
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:43,640
The modern-day equivalent of $50 billion, nearly twice that of landing a man on the moon

39
00:02:43,641 --> 00:02:46,880
to build a railroad through a land just as desolate.

40
00:02:46,881 --> 00:02:50,880
In spite of the obstacles, by the end of the 19th century,

41
00:02:50,881 --> 00:02:53,880
it was a national imperative to build it.

42
00:02:53,881 --> 00:02:57,880
It sometimes took years for messages and edicts and plans

43
00:02:57,881 --> 00:03:01,880
to reach the farthest edges of their empire.

44
00:03:01,881 --> 00:03:04,880
Without lines of communication,

45
00:03:04,881 --> 00:03:07,880
without an effective internal telegraph system,

46
00:03:07,881 --> 00:03:10,880
without a railroad system, Russia was at a serious disadvantage.

47
00:03:10,881 --> 00:03:14,880
The country occupies the largest area on Earth.

48
00:03:14,881 --> 00:03:17,880
It stretches from St. Petersburg to the Pacific.

49
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,480
It is wider than the breadth of the United States

50
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:22,580
and Canada combined.

51
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:26,820
But only wagon trails had linked it to the capital.

52
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:29,920
Russia needed a railroad to command and control

53
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:32,440
its vast Asian territories.

54
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:35,740
There were constant reports that China planned to overrun

55
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,640
Siberia's undefended border.

56
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:42,540
Warships from Japan menaced the Russian coast.

57
00:03:42,541 --> 00:03:46,040
Even more troubling were Siberian revolutionaries

58
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:49,940
who demonstrated their desire of breaking away from the capital.

59
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:54,980
By the reign of Alexander III, Russia was adrift in crisis.

60
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,780
Czar Alexander III came into power

61
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:01,300
when his father was assassinated in 1881.

62
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:08,900
His entire reign was dedicated to the idea of Russia for the Russians,

63
00:04:08,901 --> 00:04:11,900
of establishing firmer governmental control

64
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,740
over a country that seemed to be slipping out of control.

65
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:18,680
The railroad was the answer to all of this.

66
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:20,980
It was partly military defense,

67
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:24,880
but it was also internal police measures.

68
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:27,980
Alexander III was determined to build a railroad

69
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:31,420
to move soldiers to and from the hinterland.

70
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:35,420
But he also intended to use it to colonize Siberia,

71
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:38,100
just as the American Transcontinental Railroad

72
00:04:38,101 --> 00:04:42,100
had tamed the West, Russia would tame the East.

73
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:45,900
There was nothing but scattered forts and trading posts.

74
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,840
Few people lived here except convicts in exile

75
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:51,240
and Stone Age tribes.

76
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:54,080
The Tsar wanted the same success as America's

77
00:04:54,180 --> 00:04:56,620
Transcontinental Railroad, but had none

78
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:59,520
of the economic foundation.

79
00:04:59,620 --> 00:05:02,160
One of the most profound disadvantages

80
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:04,300
that Russia faced in this project

81
00:05:04,301 --> 00:05:08,240
was a lack of entrepreneurial tradition.

82
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:10,780
This was not a challenge of technology.

83
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:12,840
They didn't have to invent anything new.

84
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:16,060
It was a challenge of financing and organization.

85
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:18,680
Russia was a feudal society.

86
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:21,520
Its railroad building experience was limited

87
00:05:21,521 --> 00:05:24,680
to a few hundred miles of track with antiquated trains.

88
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:31,540
It's one thing to project a railroad of 500 or even 1,000 miles.

89
00:05:31,541 --> 00:05:37,400
It's quite another to project a railroad that would consume unimaginable quantities of timber,

90
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:44,420
require hundreds of bridges, thousands of miles of rail, millions of tons of iron for spikes and bolts.

91
00:05:45,020 --> 00:05:50,860
Even running a few trains daily on a line 6,000 miles long is going to require thousands of cars,

92
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:58,800
hundreds of locomotives, and training a workforce in a country that didn't have a great reservoir of mechanical talent.

93
00:05:58,801 --> 00:06:06,420
No railroad in Russia or the world is as long or as complicated as the one called for in the Trans-Siberian.

94
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:15,100
It had to cross three of the world's largest rivers, the world's largest forest, the world's largest lake, in the world's worst climate.

95
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:18,880
Much of the railway was built on permafrost.

96
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:23,660
It is rock solid most of the year, but melts into craters under the stress.

97
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:26,880
They must also tunnel through a mountain chain.

98
00:06:26,881 --> 00:06:33,880
Overall, there are countless bridges over ravines and rivers.

99
00:06:33,881 --> 00:06:43,880
The Trans-Siberian Railroad was simply one of the most grandiose enterprises of the 19th

100
00:06:43,881 --> 00:06:49,880
century, maybe of all of modern history, more costly than any other peaceful undertaking

101
00:06:49,881 --> 00:06:53,880
in the history of the modern world.

102
00:06:53,881 --> 00:06:56,880
Alexander entrusted the Committee of Communication

103
00:06:56,980 --> 00:06:59,780
with the task of planning the railroad.

104
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:01,880
But the committee was stymied.

105
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:04,820
They advised the czar against it.

106
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:07,620
They didn't see that the government could afford

107
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:09,920
to build this railroad, and they were right.

108
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:12,840
There wasn't a labor force of an adequate size

109
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:15,140
in isolated Siberia.

110
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:19,140
There weren't steel mills in Siberia to supply the railroad.

111
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:21,540
There weren't supplies of coal.

112
00:07:21,541 --> 00:07:26,540
Impossible or not, the Tsar was determined to have a railroad.

113
00:07:26,541 --> 00:07:29,540
When the committee told him it couldn't be built,

114
00:07:29,541 --> 00:07:33,540
he dissolved the committee, then entrusted Sergey Vita,

115
00:07:33,541 --> 00:07:36,540
the builder of the 600-mile Odessa Line,

116
00:07:36,541 --> 00:07:40,540
to build the 6,000-mile Trans-Siberian.

117
00:07:40,541 --> 00:07:46,540
But the Tsar was naive to think a royal temper could assure success.

118
00:07:46,541 --> 00:07:49,540
A railroad is much more than trains and track.

119
00:07:49,541 --> 00:07:50,500
It's not a thing.

120
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:53,380
It's nothing that a czar can call into being

121
00:07:53,381 --> 00:07:56,140
and then expect to work like some machine

122
00:07:56,141 --> 00:07:58,840
that you throw a switch and it goes forward.

123
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:02,020
It's a complex interaction of human beings

124
00:08:02,021 --> 00:08:05,980
and parts and pieces extending over thousands of miles.

125
00:08:07,220 --> 00:08:09,980
The longest railroad in the world today

126
00:08:09,981 --> 00:08:12,980
and the biggest project of the 19th century

127
00:08:12,981 --> 00:08:16,060
began in a cloud of bureaucratic fear.

128
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:19,620
While planners searched for a miracle to build it,

129
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:22,900
the Tsar boasted it was Russia's road to power,

130
00:08:23,540 --> 00:08:25,960
the start of a new era of peace and prosperity.

131
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:29,560
The fact was, it would provoke a war

132
00:08:29,561 --> 00:08:31,600
and nearly bankrupt the country.

133
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:34,840
As the century ended, many wondered

134
00:08:34,841 --> 00:08:37,600
if the railroad was worth its enormous expense,

135
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:39,600
nearly two-thirds the state budget,

136
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
or if it was just a desperate gamble of a dynasty

137
00:08:43,001 --> 00:08:45,460
in its waning days to stay in power.

138
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:48,360
Midnight in Moscow marks the departure

139
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:50,060
of the Trans-Siberian Railroad.

140
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:58,400
A first-class ticket costs $1,200 for the eight-day trip

141
00:08:58,500 --> 00:08:59,700
to Vladivostok.

142
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:03,820
But many travel and sleep in berths for under $600.

143
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:06,240
By dawn, the train reaches the Volga

144
00:09:06,340 --> 00:09:09,020
and crosses Europe's longest river.

145
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:14,260
On the distant bank is Yaroslav and its 900-year-old cathedral.

146
00:09:14,261 --> 00:09:20,260
From here, the train continues on, bound for Siberia and beyond.

147
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:22,260
Trains and train centers like this

148
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:25,000
were once the primary target of German aircraft,

149
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:28,200
trying to cut supply lines to the front in World War II.

150
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:36,320
But Yaroslav and the Trans-Siberian were spared

151
00:09:36,420 --> 00:09:38,920
because they were out of range.

152
00:09:39,020 --> 00:09:42,120
In war, distance protected the train.

153
00:09:42,121 --> 00:09:46,120
Yet in building it, distance nearly destroyed it.

154
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:50,820
Its remote location and sheer length drove the cost so high,

155
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:56,260
the project was almost derailed before it began.

156
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:59,880
I'm sure that the Russians wanted to emulate the best.

157
00:09:59,980 --> 00:10:03,380
But as they began to price out this huge, long thing,

158
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:05,240
which was maybe three times longer

159
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:09,180
than any transcontinental railroad ever built before,

160
00:10:09,280 --> 00:10:12,120
it quickly became apparent they'd bankrupt the treasury.

161
00:10:12,121 --> 00:10:13,960
and they only get halfway.

162
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:16,320
In St. Petersburg, the railroad committee

163
00:10:16,420 --> 00:10:21,420
was grappling with the most difficult detail of all, money.

164
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:24,040
Sergei Vita, the project's point man,

165
00:10:24,140 --> 00:10:27,200
had to find a way to build the world's longest railroad

166
00:10:27,300 --> 00:10:28,640
on a budget.

167
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:31,740
He had no alternative but to cut the costs of materials

168
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:36,240
and labor and collapse the project's time to completion.

169
00:10:36,340 --> 00:10:39,820
The best railroads used a double track made of steel rails

170
00:10:39,821 --> 00:10:42,220
that weighed 90 pounds to the yard.

171
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:45,020
He would have to build a single line instead

172
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:48,920
with rails made of iron that only weighed 49 pounds.

173
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:52,260
The measure reduced costs 75%.

174
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:55,260
Next, timber, not stone and steel,

175
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:59,080
was to be used in all but the widest river crossings.

176
00:10:59,180 --> 00:11:01,280
The work had to be done by hand.

177
00:11:01,380 --> 00:11:04,280
There was no money for dynamite or machines.

178
00:11:04,380 --> 00:11:07,120
And there could be no costly surveys.

179
00:11:07,121 --> 00:11:10,920
The royal directive was, just do it.

180
00:11:11,020 --> 00:11:13,420
The government was so desperate,

181
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:19,500
it was almost policy to get it done as quickly as possible,

182
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:21,900
as cheaply as possible.

183
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,440
But at the same time, whatever it took was the rule.

184
00:11:28,900 --> 00:11:31,420
In March 1891, church bells

185
00:11:31,520 --> 00:11:34,580
heralded the 10th anniversary of Alexander's ascension

186
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:35,980
to the throne.

187
00:11:35,981 --> 00:11:38,920
It was also the day the Tsar marked to start building

188
00:11:39,020 --> 00:11:41,980
the railroad, ready or not.

189
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:44,380
In Vladivostok, the Tsarevich Nicholas

190
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:47,720
arrived to shovel the first spade full of dirt,

191
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:50,220
then attended a gala celebration honoring

192
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:52,160
the project's engineers.

193
00:11:52,260 --> 00:11:56,800
Amid music and champagne, the railroad was underway.

194
00:11:56,900 --> 00:12:00,200
Back at the capital, Vita mapped out the master plan

195
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:02,700
to complete the railroad within 10 years,

196
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:05,140
as the Tsar had ordered.

197
00:12:05,141 --> 00:12:07,600
With the construction season limited to four months

198
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:10,840
by the subzero winters, Vita had 40 months

199
00:12:10,940 --> 00:12:16,020
to build 6,000 miles of track, or 150 miles a day.

200
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:18,460
The prospect was impossible.

201
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:21,380
So he borrowed an idea from Canada's transcontinental

202
00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:25,460
railroad to build in sections simultaneously.

203
00:12:25,560 --> 00:12:28,900
Vita broke the Trans-Siberian down into six parts,

204
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,440
intending to build them all at once.

205
00:12:31,540 --> 00:12:33,640
If all went according to plan, he only

206
00:12:33,641 --> 00:12:37,020
only needed to build about 25 miles of track per month

207
00:12:37,021 --> 00:12:39,480
on each section to succeed.

208
00:12:39,481 --> 00:12:41,120
To further speed the project, he

209
00:12:41,121 --> 00:12:44,200
scheduled bridge and tunnel construction in winter,

210
00:12:44,201 --> 00:12:48,340
when crews wouldn't have to pickaxe frozen earth.

211
00:12:48,341 --> 00:12:51,680
The first segment to get started was the Usuri line,

212
00:12:51,681 --> 00:12:56,520
joining Khabarovsk to Vladivostok on the Pacific coast.

213
00:12:56,521 --> 00:12:59,160
It was the country's most urgent length.

214
00:12:59,161 --> 00:13:01,400
The army needed the train to shuttle soldiers

215
00:13:01,401 --> 00:13:03,800
to guard against invasion.

216
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:05,940
Supply ships reached the distant crews

217
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:09,340
after a 40-day voyage from the Baltic to the Pacific.

218
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:15,440
There were horrendous delays, but the real problem was labor.

219
00:13:15,540 --> 00:13:21,360
Native Siberian tribes refused to work, calling the railway

220
00:13:21,460 --> 00:13:22,760
the Iron Scar.

221
00:13:22,860 --> 00:13:27,400
Officials tried to recruit workers from Russian cities,

222
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:31,400
but none were enticed by the offer of free room and board.

223
00:13:31,401 --> 00:13:35,000
and 12 cents a day to work in Siberia.

224
00:13:35,100 --> 00:13:38,900
The government was desperate for able-bodied men.

225
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:40,600
When we built our transcontinental railroads

226
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:42,940
right after the Civil War, a lot of guys are, you know,

227
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:45,240
mustered out looking for employment.

228
00:13:45,340 --> 00:13:47,640
And wages weren't too bad.

229
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:52,360
But in Siberia, you can't afford to pay these people a lot.

230
00:13:52,460 --> 00:13:54,260
So you end up with a lot of people, I think,

231
00:13:54,360 --> 00:13:58,900
that were either prisoners or economically conscripted.

232
00:13:58,901 --> 00:14:02,100
The bulk of the workforce was Russian convicts.

233
00:14:02,200 --> 00:14:04,840
They were barefoot in summer and dressed in thick layers

234
00:14:04,940 --> 00:14:06,640
for the spring and fall.

235
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:10,840
A shaved right head indicated a murderer.

236
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:15,660
Beside convicts are free men from China, Persia, and Turkey.

237
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:18,260
The pay was 12 cents a day.

238
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:21,860
Stonemasons recruited from Italy earned a dollar.

239
00:14:21,960 --> 00:14:25,300
Other than work, the men either ate or slept.

240
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:27,460
The food was dismal.

241
00:14:27,461 --> 00:14:31,000
Cook slaughtered pack animals too weak to work to make stews

242
00:14:31,100 --> 00:14:33,060
of mule and horse meat.

243
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:37,600
At night, men slept in tents or crude bunkhouses.

244
00:14:37,700 --> 00:14:39,800
The convicts were shackled to wheelbarrows

245
00:14:39,900 --> 00:14:41,900
to prevent their escape.

246
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:46,120
At first light, everyone was back to the tracks until dark,

247
00:14:46,220 --> 00:14:50,520
usually at 10 PM in the northern latitudes of Siberia.

248
00:14:50,620 --> 00:14:54,120
The routine was repeated until the winter suspended work

249
00:14:54,220 --> 00:14:55,620
till spring.

250
00:14:55,621 --> 00:14:57,620
In the chains of Siberian prisoners,

251
00:14:57,720 --> 00:15:00,560
the railroad found the miracle it needed.

252
00:15:00,660 --> 00:15:03,620
The enemies of the state became the builders of the state's

253
00:15:03,720 --> 00:15:05,800
most important undertaking.

254
00:15:05,900 --> 00:15:08,660
Motivation consisted of unshackling a prisoner

255
00:15:08,760 --> 00:15:11,740
and taking a day off his sentence for a day of labor.

256
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:14,000
They were actually paid fairly well,

257
00:15:14,100 --> 00:15:19,440
and they were fed fairly well, and conditions were much better

258
00:15:19,540 --> 00:15:22,480
working on the railroad than they were rotting away

259
00:15:22,580 --> 00:15:24,740
inside of a prison.

260
00:15:24,741 --> 00:15:28,240
Shackles or not, the prisoners often slipped into town,

261
00:15:28,340 --> 00:15:30,340
bringing outrage from the local governor

262
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:32,640
for the mayhem they caused.

263
00:15:32,740 --> 00:15:35,880
But the railroad officials were less concerned with conduct

264
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:38,680
and more troubled by the fact that they needed more bodies

265
00:15:38,780 --> 00:15:41,400
to work every hour of daylight to the maximum.

266
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:47,000
Imperial soldiers were ordered into service,

267
00:15:47,100 --> 00:15:50,640
but refused to work beside criminals and went on strike.

268
00:15:50,740 --> 00:15:54,040
Finally, the government invited 15,000 coolies

269
00:15:54,041 --> 00:15:56,040
from China.

270
00:15:56,041 --> 00:15:59,040
The government's motley labor force pushed on.

271
00:15:59,041 --> 00:16:01,040
Construction moved at full speed.

272
00:16:01,041 --> 00:16:04,040
But by late spring, it was completely disrupted.

273
00:16:04,041 --> 00:16:08,040
The initial surveys failed to reveal a critical detail.

274
00:16:08,041 --> 00:16:12,040
The melting rivers fed by tons of winter ice and snow

275
00:16:12,041 --> 00:16:16,040
had overrun their banks by as much as 15 miles.

276
00:16:16,041 --> 00:16:19,040
In many cases, the engineers who were assigned to survey

277
00:16:19,041 --> 00:16:23,040
this 5,000 or 6,000 miles through what was often a trackless

278
00:16:23,041 --> 00:16:28,620
trackless wilderness, apparently often would decide that they just really didn't have the time

279
00:16:28,621 --> 00:16:36,220
to run every line and figure out every curve, so that in many cases the surveys were, shall we say,

280
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:42,500
imaginary, lines drawn on maps without an engineer ever setting foot in that particular forest or

281
00:16:42,501 --> 00:16:43,804
that particular step.

282
00:16:43,805 --> 00:16:48,400
Well, by the time the track layers get there, they encounter features like

283
00:16:48,401 --> 00:16:52,800
rivers or streams or hills that the surveyors had overlooked

284
00:16:52,801 --> 00:16:54,640
because they'd never been there in the first place.

285
00:16:54,641 --> 00:16:59,220
The crew hammered away at the route's 470 arduous miles.

286
00:16:59,980 --> 00:17:01,620
But there were more obstacles.

287
00:17:02,440 --> 00:17:06,080
The floods turned into swamps that bred disease.

288
00:17:07,660 --> 00:17:10,200
Siberian anthrax decimated the pack animals.

289
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:14,140
Mosquitoes transmitted fevers that incapacitated hundreds.

290
00:17:15,100 --> 00:17:18,000
Worst of all was an insect barely visible

291
00:17:18,001 --> 00:17:20,000
to the human eye.

292
00:17:20,001 --> 00:17:26,000
The Siberian gnats alone would be enough to make most modern human beings go crazy.

293
00:17:26,001 --> 00:17:30,700
They were thick, unrelenting, they would get into your clothes.

294
00:17:30,701 --> 00:17:36,880
They were a combination of vicious flea and nasty mosquito rolled up into one, and there

295
00:17:36,881 --> 00:17:39,120
were billions of them.

296
00:17:39,121 --> 00:17:46,040
It became the practice of Siberian railroad workers to literally dress themselves in netting.

297
00:17:46,041 --> 00:17:49,680
Whatever the challenges, Vita's plan was succeeding.

298
00:17:49,681 --> 00:17:53,740
The real problem was still the old problem, money.

299
00:17:53,741 --> 00:17:57,480
Even with cost cuts, the project needed more.

300
00:17:57,481 --> 00:18:00,580
To raise more funds, he kept the news about the railroad's

301
00:18:00,581 --> 00:18:04,220
difficulty and delays a state secret.

302
00:18:04,221 --> 00:18:06,360
The Trans-Siberian was accompanied by a major

303
00:18:06,361 --> 00:18:09,900
propaganda campaign at the world's fairs of the day,

304
00:18:09,901 --> 00:18:13,940
especially intended for French and other foreign investors

305
00:18:13,941 --> 00:18:17,040
to see that the country was coming alive.

306
00:18:17,041 --> 00:18:18,980
Investors heard the government stress

307
00:18:18,981 --> 00:18:22,140
all the financial advantages of a railroad linking Europe

308
00:18:22,141 --> 00:18:23,480
to Asia.

309
00:18:23,481 --> 00:18:25,520
They were shown the most opulent interiors

310
00:18:25,521 --> 00:18:27,980
of any railroad cars in the world

311
00:18:27,981 --> 00:18:32,360
and told the richest would ride these rails across Asia.

312
00:18:32,361 --> 00:18:35,060
There was no mention of the fact the railroad was built

313
00:18:35,061 --> 00:18:38,940
on substandard ties and tracks with prison labor

314
00:18:38,941 --> 00:18:42,900
and traveled at best 15 miles an hour.

315
00:18:42,901 --> 00:18:44,440
The scheme worked.

316
00:18:44,441 --> 00:18:49,000
The French added up fresh loans, but it was still not enough.

317
00:18:49,001 --> 00:18:51,540
Vita ordered the treasury to print as much money

318
00:18:51,541 --> 00:18:53,280
as the project needed.

319
00:18:53,281 --> 00:18:56,780
Whatever the debt, the railroad would pay it off.

320
00:18:56,781 --> 00:18:59,880
All Vita had to do was finish it fast.

321
00:19:05,020 --> 00:19:08,700
Five days after leaving Moscow, the Trans-Siberian Railroad

322
00:19:08,701 --> 00:19:11,160
reaches the western bank of the Angara River

323
00:19:11,161 --> 00:19:15,060
and the city of Irkutsk, 2,800 miles away.

324
00:19:15,061 --> 00:19:17,460
It is the halfway point of the journey east

325
00:19:17,461 --> 00:19:21,360
and the largest city in Siberia.

326
00:19:21,361 --> 00:19:23,860
Irkutsk is the crossroads of Central Asia

327
00:19:23,861 --> 00:19:27,160
for passengers going to Mongolia and China.

328
00:19:27,161 --> 00:19:30,120
The arrival of the Trans-Siberian a century ago

329
00:19:30,121 --> 00:19:33,060
made the city a trade center.

330
00:19:33,061 --> 00:19:36,060
The homes of rich merchants and the churches they endowed

331
00:19:36,061 --> 00:19:37,060
still stand.

332
00:19:41,160 --> 00:19:45,160
Near the train station is the city's World War II memorial.

333
00:19:45,161 --> 00:19:47,160
It's a fitting location.

334
00:19:47,161 --> 00:19:51,160
With the Nazi invasion, the Trans-Siberian became a war horse,

335
00:19:51,161 --> 00:19:55,160
moving whole armies to the front.

336
00:19:55,161 --> 00:19:58,160
In peace, it is a workhorse.

337
00:19:58,161 --> 00:20:01,160
The Trans-Siberian carries the industrial lifeblood of coal,

338
00:20:01,161 --> 00:20:05,160
oil, and gas from Asia to the west.

339
00:20:05,161 --> 00:20:09,160
The tracks are built to bear the weight of 100 loaded boxcars.

340
00:20:09,161 --> 00:20:13,660
Its engines can reach top speeds of 70 miles an hour.

341
00:20:13,760 --> 00:20:15,960
The speed and strength of the Trans-Siberian

342
00:20:16,060 --> 00:20:18,800
in the 21st century is the stuff of dreams

343
00:20:18,900 --> 00:20:21,900
to the railroad committee who built it in 1891.

344
00:20:25,820 --> 00:20:29,420
For Sergei Vita, building cheap was a necessity,

345
00:20:29,520 --> 00:20:32,420
building quick a strategy.

346
00:20:32,520 --> 00:20:34,420
The sooner the railroad was finished,

347
00:20:34,520 --> 00:20:36,260
the sooner it could earn money.

348
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:39,120
Money needed to upgrade the tracks and rail

349
00:20:39,160 --> 00:20:43,860
that by necessity had to be cheap to cover 6,000 miles.

350
00:20:43,960 --> 00:20:47,400
The linchpin in the strategy was concurrent construction,

351
00:20:47,500 --> 00:20:51,300
building the Trans-Siberian in sections all at once.

352
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:54,800
The biggest challenge in crossing the Earth's largest land mass

353
00:20:54,900 --> 00:20:57,620
was not the land, but the water.

354
00:20:57,720 --> 00:21:01,020
The supply line for building the railroad

355
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:03,520
ideally comes from both ends.

356
00:21:03,620 --> 00:21:06,520
The trains just bring it in on the tracks you've already built,

357
00:21:06,521 --> 00:21:08,320
And that's the best way to carry it.

358
00:21:08,420 --> 00:21:11,920
But the Trans-Siberian Railway was started

359
00:21:12,020 --> 00:21:13,920
at six different points.

360
00:21:14,020 --> 00:21:17,660
So what they did was bring supplies in by boat,

361
00:21:17,760 --> 00:21:20,260
which meant going up to the Arctic Sea

362
00:21:20,360 --> 00:21:24,280
and then coming up one of the rivers.

363
00:21:24,380 --> 00:21:27,080
Ships were slated to drop off construction supplies

364
00:21:27,180 --> 00:21:31,780
to crews building east and west, but they never made it.

365
00:21:31,880 --> 00:21:34,920
The Arctic Sea was a killer, unlike anything

366
00:21:34,921 --> 00:21:36,920
the railroad committee expected.

367
00:21:36,921 --> 00:21:39,920
The first supply flotilla was shipwrecked.

368
00:21:39,921 --> 00:21:43,920
Thousands of miles of tracks and tons of spikes were lost.

369
00:21:43,921 --> 00:21:45,920
The accident set construction back.

370
00:21:45,921 --> 00:21:47,920
Six months after the project began,

371
00:21:47,921 --> 00:21:51,920
it was already two years behind schedule.

372
00:21:51,921 --> 00:21:54,920
The Arctic sea was dropped as a supply route.

373
00:21:54,921 --> 00:21:58,920
The project had to wait for slow-traveling supply trains

374
00:21:58,921 --> 00:22:00,920
arriving on provisional tracks.

375
00:22:00,921 --> 00:22:02,920
Delayed but not defeated,

376
00:22:02,921 --> 00:22:06,920
Not defeated, the work pushed on.

377
00:22:06,921 --> 00:22:09,320
A year after the Arctic Sea debacle,

378
00:22:09,321 --> 00:22:12,300
the project was resupplied with rails.

379
00:22:12,301 --> 00:22:14,700
Crews at the westernmost edge of Siberia

380
00:22:14,701 --> 00:22:18,260
forged eastward across the Barabinskaya steppe.

381
00:22:18,261 --> 00:22:21,880
In stretches of permafrost, crews built innumerable bonfires

382
00:22:21,881 --> 00:22:26,640
to melt the ice-hard soil and sink the track into solid ground.

383
00:22:26,641 --> 00:22:28,780
Dynamite would have made it easier,

384
00:22:28,781 --> 00:22:30,780
but it wasn't in the budget.

385
00:22:30,781 --> 00:22:38,280
The work of most Siberian track builders was repetitive and very difficult.

386
00:22:38,281 --> 00:22:44,680
They didn't have steam shovels, they didn't have access to what we would regard as modern hand tools.

387
00:22:44,681 --> 00:22:49,080
They were shaping the earth in the profile for the track,

388
00:22:49,081 --> 00:22:54,280
and doing so with wooden shovels with rakes, with small light picks.

389
00:22:54,281 --> 00:22:56,980
And in some cases, they didn't even have wheelbarrows.

390
00:22:56,981 --> 00:23:01,140
They would drag the soil on barrows to where they needed it.

391
00:23:01,700 --> 00:23:07,140
On September 11, 1894, three and a half years after the start of the project,

392
00:23:07,580 --> 00:23:08,920
crews reached the Aub River.

393
00:23:09,520 --> 00:23:11,500
It is one of the world's largest rivers.

394
00:23:12,460 --> 00:23:17,100
While crews with supplies ferried across the river to continue building east,

395
00:23:17,540 --> 00:23:18,700
masons began work.

396
00:23:20,640 --> 00:23:22,540
Slowly, the bridges took shape,

397
00:23:22,541 --> 00:23:26,160
with stone piers and thick buttresses that slanted outward

398
00:23:26,161 --> 00:23:28,880
to deflect the tons of ice that swept downriver

399
00:23:28,881 --> 00:23:31,140
at a crushing 6 miles per hour.

400
00:23:31,460 --> 00:23:34,600
But the upper section of the bridge was delayed for months.

401
00:23:35,500 --> 00:23:38,440
Every stick of rail, every nut and bolt for the bridges,

402
00:23:38,720 --> 00:23:41,700
every piece of metal had to be hauled in from workshops

403
00:23:41,701 --> 00:23:46,140
in Moscow or Warsaw or other parts of European Russia.

404
00:23:46,960 --> 00:23:49,200
The bridge beams, bearings, and rivets

405
00:23:49,201 --> 00:23:51,320
were packaged into hundreds of boxcars

406
00:23:51,321 --> 00:23:54,720
and shipped over months like pieces of a puzzle.

407
00:23:54,820 --> 00:23:56,560
The supply trains traveled at speeds

408
00:23:56,660 --> 00:23:59,560
no better than a walking pace.

409
00:23:59,660 --> 00:24:02,560
Bridge construction took place through the winter.

410
00:24:02,660 --> 00:24:06,960
It was a ruthless schedule with deadly consequences.

411
00:24:07,060 --> 00:24:09,580
But you've got problems there with working in the extreme

412
00:24:09,680 --> 00:24:10,680
cold.

413
00:24:10,780 --> 00:24:12,980
Even with gloves, your hands get cold.

414
00:24:13,080 --> 00:24:15,220
You step back to do something, and you

415
00:24:15,320 --> 00:24:16,620
miss your step on this bridge.

416
00:24:16,720 --> 00:24:18,580
You grab for something, and it's not there,

417
00:24:18,680 --> 00:24:20,280
or your hand isn't working.

418
00:24:20,281 --> 00:24:22,320
All of a sudden, there you are down in the river.

419
00:24:22,680 --> 00:24:24,980
And it's not a gentle splash that season of the year.

420
00:24:25,060 --> 00:24:25,820
It's a thud.

421
00:24:26,260 --> 00:24:29,340
The Russians stoically accepted the loss of 10 men

422
00:24:29,341 --> 00:24:31,860
for every river crossing and pushed on.

423
00:24:32,760 --> 00:24:36,160
After the bridge was finished came the real dangerous job,

424
00:24:36,840 --> 00:24:37,540
testing it.

425
00:24:38,240 --> 00:24:40,160
Engineers drove four locomotives

426
00:24:40,161 --> 00:24:43,440
with four loaded freight cars halfway across the span.

427
00:24:44,040 --> 00:24:46,940
Barring its collapse, they remained there for two hours

428
00:24:46,941 --> 00:24:49,040
while structural bend was measured.

429
00:24:49,041 --> 00:24:55,100
Then they crossed back and forth, first slowly, then to 15 miles per hour.

430
00:24:55,600 --> 00:24:57,560
The Aub River Bridge held.

431
00:24:58,400 --> 00:25:01,560
Siberia had made a joke out of building quick.

432
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:05,540
Vast distances and harsh seas had stalled the supply lines.

433
00:25:05,980 --> 00:25:09,620
It also brought up the worst of engineering based on economy.

434
00:25:10,100 --> 00:25:16,320
Typically, 3,000 ties were used to a mile, but the Trans-Siberian used 2,000 to save money.

435
00:25:16,321 --> 00:25:20,660
When combined with a light iron rail, the result was disaster.

436
00:25:20,860 --> 00:25:24,860
If you build a light railroad, you need lots of cross ties that hold it up.

437
00:25:25,080 --> 00:25:29,980
This light rail has to bridge from tie to tie, and you get an engine on it.

438
00:25:30,140 --> 00:25:31,520
It's going to sag and stretch.

439
00:25:31,800 --> 00:25:34,500
Also, Siberia has a very severe climate,

440
00:25:34,660 --> 00:25:37,180
and there's a temperature point at which steel becomes brittle,

441
00:25:37,880 --> 00:25:39,700
and they hit that in Siberia.

442
00:25:39,980 --> 00:25:42,860
So the rails would just pop apart and crumble.

443
00:25:42,861 --> 00:25:46,860
The broken pieces of the railroad were patched back together

444
00:25:46,960 --> 00:25:48,460
and work resumed.

445
00:25:48,560 --> 00:25:53,700
Even a bad train, argued Vita, is better than no train.

446
00:25:53,800 --> 00:25:57,200
But in October, tragedy struck.

447
00:25:57,300 --> 00:26:01,220
Alexander III, the man who fired a committee for telling him

448
00:26:01,320 --> 00:26:03,620
the Trans-Siberian could not be built,

449
00:26:03,720 --> 00:26:05,720
died in a train derailment.

450
00:26:08,820 --> 00:26:10,720
Sergei Vita was made a scapegoat

451
00:26:10,721 --> 00:26:14,460
a scapegoat and forced to resign by his enemies.

452
00:26:14,461 --> 00:26:18,020
The railroad had lost its two greatest champions.

453
00:26:18,021 --> 00:26:20,960
It fell on Nicholas to complete his father's vision.

454
00:26:24,760 --> 00:26:28,680
The summer in Siberia is a gift of warmth and light.

455
00:26:28,681 --> 00:26:32,480
In Urkursk, people spend long evenings by the Angara River,

456
00:26:32,481 --> 00:26:35,720
mindful that these are the best days of the year.

457
00:26:35,721 --> 00:26:39,780
In six months, temperatures will drop to 10 degrees below zero,

458
00:26:39,781 --> 00:26:43,320
and the sun will set at 3 o'clock.

459
00:26:43,420 --> 00:26:47,280
But winter is Siberia's defining season.

460
00:26:47,380 --> 00:26:50,320
For the train to succeed, it had to conquer the climate

461
00:26:50,420 --> 00:26:54,520
and sheer size of the continent.

462
00:26:54,620 --> 00:27:00,340
By the fall of 1898, it was better than halfway to victory.

463
00:27:00,440 --> 00:27:05,280
The Trans-Siberian reached Irkutsk in September 1898.

464
00:27:05,380 --> 00:27:08,040
Seven years after the project began,

465
00:27:08,041 --> 00:27:11,940
the largest city in Siberia was linked to the capital.

466
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:14,840
The distance of 2,800 miles was longer

467
00:27:14,940 --> 00:27:17,480
than the American Transcontinental Railroad,

468
00:27:17,580 --> 00:27:22,980
but still less than halfway across Siberia.

469
00:27:23,080 --> 00:27:26,360
In St. Petersburg, Tsar Nicholas honored his late father

470
00:27:26,460 --> 00:27:29,120
with the title Most Auguste Founder of the Great

471
00:27:29,220 --> 00:27:32,360
Siberian Railway and erected a statue in his memory.

472
00:27:32,361 --> 00:27:36,160
The progress was a major achievement,

473
00:27:36,260 --> 00:27:40,360
not because new technologies had been invented

474
00:27:40,460 --> 00:27:43,300
or a thorny engineering problem solved.

475
00:27:43,400 --> 00:27:46,000
The Trans-Siberians' heroic distinction

476
00:27:46,100 --> 00:27:50,020
is that a poor country succeeded in building a railway

477
00:27:50,120 --> 00:27:54,120
across Siberia, albeit one that was markedly behind the

478
00:27:54,220 --> 00:27:56,920
technological standard of the day.

479
00:27:57,020 --> 00:28:00,960
They were building a railroad by hand

480
00:28:00,961 --> 00:28:07,560
hand across the subarctic Siberia at the same time that the Wright brothers were dreaming

481
00:28:07,561 --> 00:28:11,086
of powered flight, at the very same time that in the U.S.

482
00:28:11,087 --> 00:28:12,880
the New York Central Railroad

483
00:28:12,881 --> 00:28:14,420
exceeded 100 miles an hour.

484
00:28:14,800 --> 00:28:21,180
The Russians are aspiring to a railroad that would operate at 10 or 15 miles an hour, take

485
00:28:21,181 --> 00:28:22,820
several weeks to cross the continent.

486
00:28:24,100 --> 00:28:30,000
Far from the royal celebration, the real heroes, the men who laid the tracks, continued to

487
00:28:30,001 --> 00:28:33,200
unheralded and invisible to the national eye.

488
00:28:33,300 --> 00:28:36,140
They drove the railroad forward day after day,

489
00:28:36,240 --> 00:28:39,340
overcoming whatever is in the railroad's path.

490
00:28:39,440 --> 00:28:42,540
That included a forest as large as the continental United

491
00:28:42,640 --> 00:28:43,640
States.

492
00:28:43,740 --> 00:28:46,340
The birch, pine, and spruce were so dense,

493
00:28:46,440 --> 00:28:49,760
sunlight had never reached the ground.

494
00:28:49,860 --> 00:28:52,960
When the sun finally touched the frozen forest floor,

495
00:28:53,060 --> 00:28:55,660
it turned it into a boundless swamp.

496
00:28:55,760 --> 00:28:58,960
The men had no choice but to work in hip, deep water

497
00:28:58,961 --> 00:29:02,900
and wallow in mud until drainage canals were built.

498
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:06,740
The work moved slowly, but it never stopped.

499
00:29:06,840 --> 00:29:09,340
By 1900, the railroad was finished,

500
00:29:09,440 --> 00:29:13,480
except for a final link less than 200 miles long.

501
00:29:13,580 --> 00:29:16,980
The last stretch, joining the western and eastern sections,

502
00:29:17,080 --> 00:29:19,520
was in Central Asia at Lake Baikal,

503
00:29:19,620 --> 00:29:23,120
the world's largest body of fresh water.

504
00:29:23,220 --> 00:29:27,420
The lake is 400 miles long and 60 miles across.

505
00:29:27,421 --> 00:29:32,220
It also reaches depths of over a mile, making it the deepest lake on Earth.

506
00:29:33,260 --> 00:29:37,560
Cliffs and mountains rim the southern and eastern shores like the teeth of a shark.

507
00:29:38,860 --> 00:29:41,880
The landscape was a construction crew's nightmare.

508
00:29:43,020 --> 00:29:49,580
Of the route's 6,000 miles, most had crossed Great Plains like the American Midwest or forests and swamps.

509
00:29:49,920 --> 00:29:54,240
But this section was pain and heartache embodied in every mile.

510
00:29:54,241 --> 00:30:01,200
Out of this terrain, crews had to blast a shelf wide enough for the tracks and tunnel through a mountain chain.

511
00:30:01,880 --> 00:30:07,200
The cost was $15 million, six times that of an average mile.

512
00:30:09,340 --> 00:30:12,120
The Railroad Committee's decision was unanimous.

513
00:30:12,680 --> 00:30:14,580
There had to be an easier way.

514
00:30:15,960 --> 00:30:22,980
The Railroad Committee decided to avoid the route through the mountains and build a train ferry to cross the waters of Lake Baikal.

515
00:30:22,981 --> 00:30:25,980
The idea is copied from the Americans.

516
00:30:25,981 --> 00:30:28,980
This is not an unusual thing to do.

517
00:30:28,981 --> 00:30:31,980
It's been used, it was used a lot in the United States

518
00:30:31,981 --> 00:30:34,980
in the early days, even until the 1970s,

519
00:30:34,981 --> 00:30:36,980
to get trains across Lake Michigan

520
00:30:36,981 --> 00:30:39,980
to avoid the congestion of Chicago.

521
00:30:39,981 --> 00:30:43,980
But at Lake Baikal, what they did was propose a ferry

522
00:30:43,981 --> 00:30:46,980
to take the train across because they would be quicker

523
00:30:46,981 --> 00:30:48,980
than to build the line all the way around

524
00:30:48,981 --> 00:30:50,980
the south end of the lake.

525
00:30:50,981 --> 00:30:54,980
The vessel that would cross the lake was named the Baikal.

526
00:30:55,080 --> 00:30:56,980
It was designed and built in England,

527
00:30:57,080 --> 00:31:00,980
297 feet long by 57 feet wide.

528
00:31:01,080 --> 00:31:05,020
It had twin propellers, an inch thick steel hull reinforced

529
00:31:05,120 --> 00:31:07,020
by a two-foot inner sheathing of timber,

530
00:31:07,120 --> 00:31:10,640
and a bow propeller to shear away ice.

531
00:31:10,740 --> 00:31:13,640
When the ship was finished, it broke through ice four feet

532
00:31:13,740 --> 00:31:15,040
thick.

533
00:31:15,140 --> 00:31:17,640
It was one of the biggest and most powerful train ferries

534
00:31:17,740 --> 00:31:19,040
in the world.

535
00:31:19,041 --> 00:31:23,580
built to carry 1,200 passengers, 28 fully loaded boxcars,

536
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:28,280
and 12 locomotives at a speed of 13 knots.

537
00:31:28,380 --> 00:31:31,080
Three rows of tracks rested above the ship's keel

538
00:31:31,180 --> 00:31:33,880
so a train could drive on and off the ferry.

539
00:31:33,980 --> 00:31:35,760
It was the one state-of-the-art item

540
00:31:35,860 --> 00:31:38,700
on the Trans-Siberian Railway.

541
00:31:38,800 --> 00:31:42,200
A smaller icebreaker and passenger ferry named the Angara

542
00:31:42,300 --> 00:31:43,300
was also built.

543
00:31:43,400 --> 00:31:45,700
Its job was to plow ahead of the Baikal,

544
00:31:45,800 --> 00:31:47,740
breaking a path for it.

545
00:31:47,741 --> 00:31:50,740
The train ferry was finished in 12 months,

546
00:31:50,840 --> 00:31:55,040
but the only way to get the vessel to Siberia and Lake Baikal

547
00:31:55,140 --> 00:31:59,780
was to break it down, box it, ship it by boat, then rail,

548
00:31:59,880 --> 00:32:03,680
and reassemble it at its new port, Litvianca.

549
00:32:03,780 --> 00:32:06,100
Reaching a landlocked port in Central Asia

550
00:32:06,200 --> 00:32:08,500
proved to be more difficult than construction.

551
00:32:08,600 --> 00:32:12,400
The Baikal was delayed by two years when its supply train

552
00:32:12,500 --> 00:32:15,640
had to wait until a bridge was constructed.

553
00:32:15,641 --> 00:32:19,240
In the drawn-out delay, parts were lost in transit.

554
00:32:19,340 --> 00:32:21,280
Then it was discovered that some ship pieces

555
00:32:21,380 --> 00:32:23,880
were too big to fit on the supply train

556
00:32:23,980 --> 00:32:28,080
and had to be rebuilt, causing a further loss of time.

557
00:32:28,180 --> 00:32:29,780
It had 15 boilers.

558
00:32:29,880 --> 00:32:31,480
The reason for that is that they couldn't have

559
00:32:31,580 --> 00:32:34,000
any one of these boilers any bigger than would fit on a

560
00:32:34,100 --> 00:32:37,000
railroad car to carry it down the track and get it to Irkutsk

561
00:32:37,100 --> 00:32:39,300
and finally get it to the place where the boat was being

562
00:32:39,400 --> 00:32:40,300
assembled.

563
00:32:40,400 --> 00:32:42,740
Three more years passed before the ship was finally

564
00:32:42,741 --> 00:32:48,240
finally reassembled and put into service. But the waste of time was a minor

565
00:32:48,241 --> 00:32:52,240
inconvenience compared to what was in store for the Baikal.

566
00:32:52,241 --> 00:32:57,380
Perhaps one of the biggest surprises in all of Siberia was Lake Baikal. Even the

567
00:32:57,381 --> 00:33:02,240
Russians underestimated winter in Siberia, winter on Lake Baikal. The lake

568
00:33:02,241 --> 00:33:08,260
seemed to have a life of its own. It had its own weather, it had its own moods, it

569
00:33:08,261 --> 00:33:11,760
It had its own hazards and its own beauties.

570
00:33:11,761 --> 00:33:14,200
Storms could come up almost instantly,

571
00:33:14,201 --> 00:33:17,160
storms large enough to throw this immense car ferry,

572
00:33:17,161 --> 00:33:21,500
this iron boat, big enough to sail any ocean in the world,

573
00:33:21,501 --> 00:33:25,280
far enough off course and stop it in its wake.

574
00:33:25,281 --> 00:33:27,340
After one year on the lake,

575
00:33:27,341 --> 00:33:30,120
the Baikal proved itself useless.

576
00:33:30,121 --> 00:33:31,980
It was a total loss.

577
00:33:31,981 --> 00:33:35,520
In spring, summer, and fall, storms or a fog

578
00:33:35,521 --> 00:33:38,020
constantly held the Baikal in port.

579
00:33:38,021 --> 00:33:40,860
In winter, it was unable to break through the ice.

580
00:33:40,960 --> 00:33:43,920
The hydrographers assigned to measure the thickness of the ice

581
00:33:44,020 --> 00:33:45,820
had drawn incorrect measurements.

582
00:33:45,920 --> 00:33:50,100
In the dead of winter, the ice rose to nine feet, not four.

583
00:33:50,200 --> 00:33:53,340
In feudal desperation, men were sent onto the ice

584
00:33:53,440 --> 00:33:55,300
to hack a path for the ferry.

585
00:33:55,400 --> 00:33:56,440
But it was useless.

586
00:33:56,540 --> 00:33:59,900
This whole huge bloody ship slides up on top of the ice,

587
00:34:00,000 --> 00:34:02,920
fails to break through, slides back.

588
00:34:03,020 --> 00:34:05,440
I mean, it just flat doesn't work.

589
00:34:05,441 --> 00:34:08,340
And so passengers and cargoes and stuff

590
00:34:08,341 --> 00:34:10,340
are piling up at either side of the lake.

591
00:34:10,880 --> 00:34:12,380
You just physically can't get across.

592
00:34:12,580 --> 00:34:15,700
It's just, you know, a bloody, ill-conceived mess.

593
00:34:16,160 --> 00:34:19,480
Your whole railroad from Moscow to Vladivostok

594
00:34:19,481 --> 00:34:20,860
ceases to be able to run.

595
00:34:21,980 --> 00:34:24,340
Lake Baikal had the final word.

596
00:34:25,080 --> 00:34:26,860
The ferries were abandoned in a year,

597
00:34:26,861 --> 00:34:30,260
a frost-rimmed memento of Siberia's power

598
00:34:30,261 --> 00:34:31,760
over men and machines.

599
00:34:32,540 --> 00:34:34,540
The railroad committee had no alternative

600
00:34:34,541 --> 00:34:38,920
but to build a 162-mile track through the mountains and cliffs

601
00:34:38,921 --> 00:34:39,840
above the lake.

602
00:34:40,640 --> 00:34:43,800
The undertaking rivaled the most difficult stretch of track

603
00:34:43,801 --> 00:34:44,520
ever built.

604
00:34:45,320 --> 00:34:47,820
It required no less than 38 tunnels

605
00:34:47,821 --> 00:34:49,740
through sheer granite mountains.

606
00:34:50,880 --> 00:34:53,960
The only relief was that at long last,

607
00:34:53,961 --> 00:34:56,880
the Trans-Siberian was almost finished.

608
00:34:58,580 --> 00:35:01,320
Vladivostok, Russia's easternmost city,

609
00:35:01,321 --> 00:35:05,160
6,000 miles and eight time zones from Moscow.

610
00:35:05,260 --> 00:35:07,420
It is the hub of the Pacific fleet,

611
00:35:07,520 --> 00:35:10,860
even though the warships are less at sea and more at anchor

612
00:35:10,960 --> 00:35:12,360
in the post-Cold War era.

613
00:35:15,500 --> 00:35:18,740
In Soviet times, it was a restricted city.

614
00:35:18,840 --> 00:35:20,840
Without government permission, no one

615
00:35:20,940 --> 00:35:23,300
was allowed to enter or leave.

616
00:35:23,400 --> 00:35:25,620
But those days are over.

617
00:35:25,720 --> 00:35:28,580
The Trans-Siberian Railroad reaches Vladivostok,

618
00:35:28,581 --> 00:35:31,980
uninspected by secret police eight days after it leaves

619
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:33,380
the capital.

620
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:36,180
It arrives at the station where young Prince Nicholas

621
00:35:36,280 --> 00:35:41,620
inaugurated the project in 1891 amid so much hope and fanfare.

622
00:35:41,720 --> 00:35:44,200
A decade after construction began,

623
00:35:44,300 --> 00:35:46,740
the Trans-Siberian was once again steeped

624
00:35:46,840 --> 00:35:50,340
in excited anticipation.

625
00:35:50,440 --> 00:35:53,540
Crews prepared to drive the railroad to completion.

626
00:35:53,640 --> 00:35:56,240
But first, they had to challenge the formidable cliffs

627
00:35:56,241 --> 00:36:00,620
and mountains around it, turning rock into a railway 162 miles

628
00:36:00,720 --> 00:36:02,980
long.

629
00:36:03,080 --> 00:36:06,280
This section of track surpassed any of the challenges that once

630
00:36:06,380 --> 00:36:08,080
confronted the builders of America's

631
00:36:08,180 --> 00:36:11,520
Transcontinental Railroad.

632
00:36:11,620 --> 00:36:15,060
Not only was it more difficult by virtue of the landscape,

633
00:36:15,160 --> 00:36:19,460
it had none of America's state-of-the-art equipment.

634
00:36:19,560 --> 00:36:22,400
The most difficult construction on the Trans-Siberian,

635
00:36:22,500 --> 00:36:24,960
and arguably in all of railroading,

636
00:36:24,961 --> 00:36:28,600
had to rely instead on brute strength, determination,

637
00:36:28,700 --> 00:36:30,960
and hand tools.

638
00:36:31,060 --> 00:36:34,700
That's all crews had to build 38 tunnels through a mountain

639
00:36:34,800 --> 00:36:39,500
chain that rises 1,500 feet above a lake the size of an ocean

640
00:36:39,600 --> 00:36:41,840
in central Siberia.

641
00:36:41,940 --> 00:36:45,580
Elsewhere in the world, at the beginning of the 20th century,

642
00:36:45,680 --> 00:36:48,220
tunneling was considered high technology.

643
00:36:48,320 --> 00:36:49,720
There were pneumatic drills.

644
00:36:49,820 --> 00:36:52,080
There were high explosives.

645
00:36:52,180 --> 00:36:54,420
There were tunneling machines invented in Europe

646
00:36:54,421 --> 00:37:00,020
in the United States that often made fairly short work of solid rock.

647
00:37:00,021 --> 00:37:06,100
Russia built these tunnels the hard way, the old-fashioned way, with hand drills, with

648
00:37:06,101 --> 00:37:13,580
sweaty men with hammers working inside cramped rocky tombs, making holes in the rock into

649
00:37:13,581 --> 00:37:18,780
which the blasters would tamp their powder, insert the dynamite sticks, and light the

650
00:37:18,781 --> 00:37:21,320
fuses and run as fast as they could.

651
00:37:21,321 --> 00:37:24,420
This was tunneling 1850 style.

652
00:37:24,421 --> 00:37:27,820
The trouble was this was the early 20th century.

653
00:37:27,821 --> 00:37:30,560
In spite of the hardships in building the link past Lake

654
00:37:30,561 --> 00:37:34,460
Baikal, the railroad inched onward to completion.

655
00:37:34,461 --> 00:37:38,840
Builders estimated the railroad's finale in the spring of 1904,

656
00:37:38,841 --> 00:37:43,940
14 years after it began in Vladivostok.

657
00:37:43,941 --> 00:37:48,040
In St. Petersburg, Tsar Nicholas II eagerly awaited the train's

658
00:37:48,041 --> 00:37:52,040
with all the pride and excitement of a new father.

659
00:37:52,140 --> 00:37:54,680
With the last mile only months away,

660
00:37:54,780 --> 00:37:58,380
he prepared a celebration, boasting to a world audience

661
00:37:58,480 --> 00:38:03,080
that the railroad had bridled Asia in steam and iron at last.

662
00:38:03,180 --> 00:38:05,700
He also lavished the railroad in bravado,

663
00:38:05,800 --> 00:38:08,700
flaunting it as a tool of empire and bragging

664
00:38:08,800 --> 00:38:11,800
it marked a new era of Russian military prowess

665
00:38:11,900 --> 00:38:13,700
in the Far East.

666
00:38:13,800 --> 00:38:15,900
The Tsar would have been better off

667
00:38:15,901 --> 00:38:18,300
better off to say nothing at all.

668
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:20,940
His unmeasured speech and lack of tact

669
00:38:21,040 --> 00:38:23,540
sparked a cataclysmic disaster.

670
00:38:23,640 --> 00:38:27,340
On February 8, 1904, Japan launched a sneak attack

671
00:38:27,440 --> 00:38:30,940
on Russia's Pacific coast to destroy the Trans-Siberian

672
00:38:31,040 --> 00:38:35,020
Railroad and break St. Petersburg's link to the east.

673
00:38:35,120 --> 00:38:38,060
The Japanese deliberately struck in the dead of winter

674
00:38:38,160 --> 00:38:40,300
when the train was most vulnerable.

675
00:38:40,400 --> 00:38:42,920
But to Russia and the Tsar's horror,

676
00:38:42,921 --> 00:38:45,960
The Trans-Siberian was not just vulnerable up and down

677
00:38:46,060 --> 00:38:47,860
at 6,000 miles.

678
00:38:47,960 --> 00:38:49,720
It was a death trap.

679
00:38:49,820 --> 00:38:52,800
Its light rails broke down, unable to bear the weight

680
00:38:52,900 --> 00:38:55,860
of boxcars overloaded with weapons and soldiers.

681
00:38:55,960 --> 00:38:58,600
There was a great line about the Trans-Siberian that kind of got

682
00:38:58,700 --> 00:39:01,340
coined fairly early, and that is a first rate, third rate

683
00:39:01,440 --> 00:39:02,800
railroad.

684
00:39:02,900 --> 00:39:06,140
And the third rate part meant just everything was cheap.

685
00:39:06,240 --> 00:39:09,080
They would begin to do the grading instead of a sturdy

686
00:39:09,180 --> 00:39:10,240
basis.

687
00:39:10,340 --> 00:39:11,480
You know, it would be a pile of dirt.

688
00:39:11,481 --> 00:39:16,480
And the cheap green wood for the ties,

689
00:39:16,580 --> 00:39:19,980
pretty cheap and soft iron for the rails.

690
00:39:20,080 --> 00:39:21,520
And you get a railroad for a while,

691
00:39:21,620 --> 00:39:25,320
and then come derailments, come washouts.

692
00:39:25,420 --> 00:39:29,000
I mean, there was a derailment every few weeks.

693
00:39:29,100 --> 00:39:31,360
The policy of building quick and cheap

694
00:39:31,460 --> 00:39:33,600
was a disaster in the exigency of war.

695
00:39:36,300 --> 00:39:38,580
They built it, first of all, single-tracked

696
00:39:38,680 --> 00:39:41,480
to save money, with the result that during the war

697
00:39:41,481 --> 00:39:46,280
they couldn't send troops to China at the same time

698
00:39:46,380 --> 00:39:50,120
that they were bringing wounded troops out of China.

699
00:39:50,220 --> 00:39:52,320
The lines were constantly clogged.

700
00:39:52,420 --> 00:39:54,520
Within 10 years of the initial construction,

701
00:39:54,620 --> 00:39:57,220
they began reconstructing the entire railroad.

702
00:39:57,320 --> 00:39:59,640
The war ended a year later in defeat

703
00:39:59,740 --> 00:40:02,140
for the Tsar and the railroad.

704
00:40:02,240 --> 00:40:05,000
The Trans-Siberian was blamed as one of the reasons

705
00:40:05,100 --> 00:40:07,240
for Russia's ignominious defeat.

706
00:40:07,340 --> 00:40:10,640
A painstaking process of rebuilding began.

707
00:40:10,641 --> 00:40:15,140
Finally, by 1916, the railroad was finished.

708
00:40:15,240 --> 00:40:18,880
Russia had its long-desired transcontinental railroad,

709
00:40:18,980 --> 00:40:20,680
but there was no celebration.

710
00:40:20,780 --> 00:40:23,080
Well, I suppose in one way you could call building this railroad

711
00:40:23,180 --> 00:40:24,280
like the 30 Years' War.

712
00:40:24,380 --> 00:40:25,480
It's a great dream.

713
00:40:25,580 --> 00:40:28,800
It obviously has lots of aspects of empire.

714
00:40:28,900 --> 00:40:32,100
But in the end, it becomes this connection

715
00:40:32,200 --> 00:40:35,440
between a nation whose geography is so vast

716
00:40:35,540 --> 00:40:38,440
that by the time they finally get to the end a decade later,

717
00:40:38,441 --> 00:40:40,440
Everyone is just exhausted.

718
00:40:40,540 --> 00:40:44,840
The railroad and the war depleted the national treasury.

719
00:40:44,940 --> 00:40:47,780
There was no capital to fund social programs,

720
00:40:47,880 --> 00:40:51,080
making bad times in Russia worse.

721
00:40:51,180 --> 00:40:54,220
It also prevented the government from modernizing the army.

722
00:40:54,320 --> 00:40:57,720
When World War I broke out, Russia was ill-equipped

723
00:40:57,820 --> 00:41:00,520
and suffered horrible casualties.

724
00:41:00,620 --> 00:41:03,800
The troubles at the front, its problems at home,

725
00:41:03,900 --> 00:41:06,100
led to an insurmountable crisis.

726
00:41:06,101 --> 00:41:09,100
and ultimately the Russian Revolution.

727
00:41:09,200 --> 00:41:12,500
The promise of the railroad to bring peace and prosperity,

728
00:41:12,600 --> 00:41:15,940
once used to justify its enormous ambition,

729
00:41:16,040 --> 00:41:18,040
went unfulfilled.

730
00:41:18,140 --> 00:41:21,240
There was almost a utopian vision behind this railroad

731
00:41:21,340 --> 00:41:23,560
that would solve all of the problems of Russia,

732
00:41:23,660 --> 00:41:27,560
that it would bring Russia into the center of world trade.

733
00:41:27,660 --> 00:41:29,760
And none of it came to pass.

734
00:41:29,860 --> 00:41:32,400
Nicholas II, who proudly inaugurated

735
00:41:32,500 --> 00:41:35,260
the railroad's construction, had his final glimpse

736
00:41:35,261 --> 00:41:39,355
final glimpse of St. Petersburg from a window on the train.

737
00:41:39,356 --> 00:41:42,340
Under cover of darkness, the Trans-Siberian

738
00:41:42,341 --> 00:41:48,560
Railroad, once the hope of the Romanov family, carried Nicholas II to his death in Siberia.

739
00:41:49,380 --> 00:41:55,260
In years that followed, the Tsar's once rickety train grew into a mighty railroad when money and

740
00:41:55,261 --> 00:41:57,998
time were added to improve it.

741
00:41:57,999 --> 00:42:03,840
In the Russian Civil War, the Communists used the Trans-Siberian

742
00:42:03,841 --> 00:42:06,880
to consolidate power across the country.

743
00:42:06,881 --> 00:42:09,540
During World War II, it rushed fresh soldiers

744
00:42:09,541 --> 00:42:12,040
to the battlefronts, turning the tide of the war

745
00:42:12,041 --> 00:42:14,520
against Hitler.

746
00:42:14,521 --> 00:42:18,180
In the 1950s, the industrial might of the Soviet Union

747
00:42:18,181 --> 00:42:21,800
turned the railroad into a state-of-the-art masterpiece.

748
00:42:21,801 --> 00:42:24,260
By the end of the decade, the communists

749
00:42:24,261 --> 00:42:27,520
upgraded the Trans-Siberian with heavy steel rails,

750
00:42:27,521 --> 00:42:32,200
new cross ties, new bridges, and a second track.

751
00:42:32,201 --> 00:42:35,740
The result is one of the world's strongest and most durable railways.

752
00:42:36,100 --> 00:42:42,820
This was in every way a handmade, hard-won, difficult project

753
00:42:42,821 --> 00:42:46,620
that took a toll in time and money and blood.

754
00:42:48,300 --> 00:42:52,800
The railway the Tsar desperately wanted but could barely afford

755
00:42:52,801 --> 00:42:57,820
